1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,463
A merge is red: Git has stopped on a conflict,
two edits it can't combine by itself.

2
00:00:09,503 --> 00:00:14,466
An engineer searches for a way to "take our
side", finds two commands that differ by

3
00:00:14,506 --> 00:00:18,575
one letter, and uses the first one
that makes the conflict disappear.

4
00:00:18,915 --> 00:00:23,820
One of those commands merges everything the
other branch did, except the hunks that

5
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:28,526
conflict. The other throws away everything
the other branch did, all of it, and

6
00:00:28,566 --> 00:00:33,233
records the branch as merged, so that Git
will never offer those changes again.

7
00:00:33,273 --> 00:00:35,616
Which is which? Say your guess out loud.

8
00:00:40,456 --> 00:00:44,441
Both end with a green merge and no
message that anything was dropped.

9
00:00:44,481 --> 00:00:47,882
If you can't say which is which,
you shouldn't type either.

10
00:00:47,922 --> 00:00:52,723
That's no criticism: one letter is a thin
wall. This video separates them, and then

11
00:00:52,763 --> 00:00:57,622
answers the question underneath: under
exactly which condition do two edits conflict

12
00:00:57,662 --> 00:00:58,030
at all?

13
00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:03,704
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:03,744 --> 00:01:08,847
A merge combines two branches from three
snapshots: the base they share, ours and

15
00:01:08,887 --> 00:01:12,149
theirs. You know the rule
table from the last video.

16
00:01:12,189 --> 00:01:15,959
One of its rows said "merge the
content of the three files".

17
00:01:15,999 --> 00:01:17,610
This video opens that row.

18
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:21,606
First, the vocabulary. A merge
strategy is the algorithm.

19
00:01:21,646 --> 00:01:27,053
A strategy option adjusts one strategy. Then
three ways of "taking a side", compared

20
00:01:27,093 --> 00:01:32,370
by content and by ancestry. Then the exact
condition for a content conflict, shown

21
00:01:32,410 --> 00:01:37,168
on five pairs of edits. You'll predict
each of the five before you see it.

22
00:01:39,008 --> 00:01:40,587
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:40,927 --> 00:01:42,339
After this video you can:

24
00:01:42,679 --> 00:01:45,729
Distinguish a merge strategy
from a strategy option.

25
00:01:46,069 --> 00:01:52,084
Compare -X ours, -X theirs and -s
ours by content and by ancestry.

26
00:01:52,424 --> 00:01:56,437
State the exact condition under which
two edits to one file conflict.

27
00:01:56,777 --> 00:01:59,090
Predict for a pair of edits
whether they conflict.

28
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:06,547
Strategies and options. In one sentence:
a merge strategy is the algorithm that

29
00:02:06,587 --> 00:02:10,414
turns the inputs into a result
tree, selected with -s.

30
00:02:10,454 --> 00:02:15,283
A strategy option, passed with -X,
adjusts how one strategy behaves.

31
00:02:15,623 --> 00:02:18,838
The strategies the Git 2.55 manual lists.

32
00:02:19,178 --> 00:02:24,085
ort: in the manual's words, "the default
merge strategy when pulling or merging one

33
00:02:24,125 --> 00:02:28,853
branch". It's a three-way merge. It builds
a virtual base when there are several

34
00:02:28,893 --> 00:02:33,681
merge bases. It detects renames. And it
defaults to the histogram diff algorithm.

35
00:02:33,721 --> 00:02:38,628
You meet it in every ordinary merge, and
also under rebase, cherry-pick and revert.

36
00:02:38,968 --> 00:02:44,034
recursive: "now a synonym for ort". You
meet it in old scripts and old tutorials.

37
00:02:44,374 --> 00:02:47,750
resolve: three-way, and it
"does not handle renames".

38
00:02:47,790 --> 00:02:48,201
Legacy.

39
00:02:48,541 --> 00:02:53,164
octopus: for more than two heads. It
"refuses to do a complex merge that needs

40
00:02:53,204 --> 00:02:54,300
manual resolution".

41
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:59,096
ours: the result tree "is always that
of the current branch head, effectively

42
00:02:59,136 --> 00:03:01,782
ignoring all changes from
all other branches".

43
00:03:02,122 --> 00:03:06,583
subtree: "a modified ort strategy" that
shifts one tree to match a subdirectory of

44
00:03:06,623 --> 00:03:07,132
the other.

45
00:03:07,472 --> 00:03:12,585
The options ort accepts with -X. ours and
theirs: which side wins a conflicting

46
00:03:12,625 --> 00:03:16,563
hunk. The whitespace options,
among them ignore-space-change.

47
00:03:16,603 --> 00:03:20,738
The rename options. And diff-algorithm,
renormalize and subtree.

48
00:03:21,078 --> 00:03:25,257
Quick quiz. Back to the two
commands from the start.

49
00:03:25,297 --> 00:03:29,639
Which one throws away everything
the other branch did?

50
00:03:29,679 --> 00:03:32,560
A, -X ours. B, -s ours. Your answer?

51
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:40,126
-X ours is not -s ours.

52
00:03:40,466 --> 00:03:45,433
The answer is B. What it is: -X ours
is an option of the ort strategy.

53
00:03:45,473 --> 00:03:47,721
-s ours is a different strategy.

54
00:03:48,061 --> 00:03:51,750
Their non-conflicting changes:
with -X ours, merged.

55
00:03:51,790 --> 00:03:53,471
With -s ours, discarded.

56
00:03:53,811 --> 00:03:59,109
Conflicting hunks: with -X ours, your
side, without a conflict being reported.

57
00:03:59,149 --> 00:04:01,094
With -s ours, not applicable.

58
00:04:01,434 --> 00:04:05,670
The tree of the merge commit: with
-X ours, a real merge result.

59
00:04:05,710 --> 00:04:09,078
With -s ours, identical to
the first parent's tree.

60
00:04:09,418 --> 00:04:14,821
Legitimate use: for -X ours, rare: mechanical
conflicts where one side is known to

61
00:04:14,861 --> 00:04:20,330
be right. For -s ours: to declare a branch
obsolete, so that it never merges again.

62
00:04:20,670 --> 00:04:25,728
By ancestry, the two are the same: in both
cases their commits become ancestors of

63
00:04:25,768 --> 00:04:29,210
your branch, and git branch
--merged lists their branch.

64
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:31,386
By content, they are opposite ends.

65
00:04:31,726 --> 00:04:33,176
There is no -s theirs.

66
00:04:33,516 --> 00:04:38,106
All three forms carry the label 🟡
CAUTION in the textbook's table.

67
00:04:38,146 --> 00:04:43,579
-X ours, -X theirs and -X ignore-space-change
move the current branch like any

68
00:04:43,619 --> 00:04:47,788
merge, and silently drop one
side of every conflicting hunk.

69
00:04:47,828 --> 00:04:52,910
-s ours records the other branch as
merged and takes none of its content.

70
00:04:52,950 --> 00:04:57,541
The preview for the -X forms is to
merge without the option first.

71
00:04:57,581 --> 00:05:01,820
For -s ours, git diff HEAD...<other>
shows what is discarded.

72
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:05,556
Whitespace options. Whitespace
means spaces and tabs.

73
00:05:05,596 --> 00:05:10,484
With a whitespace option, the manual
says, "if their version only introduces

74
00:05:10,524 --> 00:05:13,790
whitespace changes to a
line, our version is used".

75
00:05:13,830 --> 00:05:19,236
So the option does not merge whitespace
changes. It discards them wherever they meet

76
00:05:19,276 --> 00:05:21,634
a line that our side kept or changed.

77
00:05:21,974 --> 00:05:26,952
Exactly why conflicts occur. In one sentence:
a content conflict occurs when both

78
00:05:26,992 --> 00:05:31,845
sides changed the same lines of a file,
or lines with no unchanged line between

79
00:05:31,885 --> 00:05:34,571
them, and did not make
the identical change.

80
00:05:34,911 --> 00:05:39,980
Precisely. For a path in the "X, Y, Z"
row, Git merges the three file contents.

81
00:05:40,020 --> 00:05:45,412
It finds what ours changed relative to the
base, and what theirs changed relative to

82
00:05:45,452 --> 00:05:50,779
the base, each as a set of hunks. A hunk
is a run of base lines that were replaced,

83
00:05:50,819 --> 00:05:52,396
removed or inserted into.

84
00:05:52,736 --> 00:05:57,666
Hunks that don't touch are both applied.
Hunks that cover the same base lines with

85
00:05:57,706 --> 00:06:01,849
the same replacement are applied
once. Everything else is a conflict.

86
00:06:01,889 --> 00:06:06,819
The manual's wording: "When both sides made
changes to the same area, however, Git

87
00:06:06,859 --> 00:06:09,608
cannot randomly pick one
side over the other".

88
00:06:09,948 --> 00:06:14,768
The unit is the line. A one-word change
in a long line conflicts with any other

89
00:06:14,808 --> 00:06:19,937
change to that line. And the test is adjacency:
one unchanged line between two edits

90
00:06:19,977 --> 00:06:21,721
is enough to keep them apart.

91
00:06:22,061 --> 00:06:25,088
What Git never does. It
never conflicts on meaning.

92
00:06:25,128 --> 00:06:30,080
Two changes that are textually far apart
and logically incompatible merge without a

93
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:30,380
word.

94
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:36,205
When not to use the options. The textbook
is direct: -X ours and -X theirs turn "Git

95
00:06:36,245 --> 00:06:39,690
stopped and asked" into "Git
chose and said nothing".

96
00:06:39,730 --> 00:06:45,215
They are defensible where one side is correct
by construction, as with a regenerated

97
00:06:45,255 --> 00:06:49,687
lock file. They are indefensible as
a way to make a red merge green.

98
00:06:51,527 --> 00:06:56,496
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy
for conflicts: two people mark up the same

99
00:06:56,536 --> 00:07:00,466
printed page. Marks in different
paragraphs can both be typed in.

100
00:07:00,506 --> 00:07:03,337
Marks in the same sentence
need a conversation.

101
00:07:03,677 --> 00:07:08,169
The analogy breaks at the boundary:
Git's unit is the line, and its test is

102
00:07:08,209 --> 00:07:12,581
adjacency, not whether the two edits
have anything to do with each other.

103
00:07:12,621 --> 00:07:15,663
Two unrelated edits on
neighbouring lines conflict.

104
00:07:15,703 --> 00:07:18,503
Two contradictory edits
ten lines apart do not.

105
00:07:18,843 --> 00:07:23,840
For the options, think of a meeting. A normal
merge is a meeting where disagreements

106
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:28,698
are put on the table. -X ours is a meeting
where, on every point of disagreement,

107
00:07:28,738 --> 00:07:33,435
your position is adopted without discussion,
and everything else the other side

108
00:07:33,475 --> 00:07:38,293
proposed is accepted. -s ours is signing
the minutes "meeting held, all proposals

109
00:07:38,333 --> 00:07:41,051
considered" without
reading a single proposal.

110
00:07:42,891 --> 00:07:47,518
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on
paper. Draw five numbered lines.

111
00:07:47,558 --> 00:07:52,703
Ours changes line 2. For theirs, try
line 3, then line 4, then line 5.

112
00:07:52,743 --> 00:07:57,518
Which of the three conflicts with
ours? Say your answer out loud.

113
00:08:02,358 --> 00:08:07,535
Five base lines. Ours changes line
2. Three possibilities for theirs.

114
00:08:07,575 --> 00:08:13,886
Line 5: far apart, both applied. Line 3: it
touches ours, and no unchanged line lies

115
00:08:13,926 --> 00:08:17,818
between them. One region,
changed by both: conflict.

116
00:08:17,858 --> 00:08:23,186
Line 4: line 3 is unchanged on both
sides, and that one line is enough.

117
00:08:23,226 --> 00:08:26,135
Both applied. So only line 3 conflicts.

118
00:08:43,975 --> 00:08:48,473
Into the lab. You changed the temperature.
Asha changed the temperature and the

119
00:08:48,513 --> 00:08:50,196
seed, and added a prompt line.

120
00:08:50,536 --> 00:08:56,075
Three things to watch after each merge: the
temperature, the seed, and the last line

121
00:08:56,115 --> 00:08:59,727
of the prompt. Predict all
three for -X ours 🟡 CAUTION.

122
00:08:59,767 --> 00:09:00,922
Say them out loud.

123
00:09:05,762 --> 00:09:10,964
A real three-way merge in which only the
conflicting hunk is decided in your favour:

124
00:09:11,004 --> 00:09:14,896
the temperature stays 0.0. Her
seed and her prompt line arrive.

125
00:09:15,236 --> 00:09:18,051
-X theirs 🟡 CAUTION is the mirror image.

126
00:09:18,391 --> 00:09:24,194
Now -s ours 🟡 CAUTION. Predict the same
three things, and one more: will git branch

127
00:09:24,234 --> 00:09:25,953
--merged list her branch?

128
00:09:30,793 --> 00:09:35,917
"Merge made by the 'ours' strategy." No seed
change, no prompt line, and the tree of

129
00:09:35,957 --> 00:09:39,728
the merge commit is identical to
the tree of its first parent.

130
00:09:39,768 --> 00:09:44,769
Yet git branch --merged lists her branch,
because her commits are now ancestors of

131
00:09:44,809 --> 00:09:48,211
main. The merge recorded
ancestry and discarded content.

132
00:09:48,251 --> 00:09:51,961
That's the one-letter difference
from the opening, on screen.

133
00:09:52,301 --> 00:09:57,242
There is no -s theirs. The list of
"available strategies" lacks ort, because it

134
00:09:57,282 --> 00:10:01,970
enumerates helper programs on disk, and
ort is built into git merge itself.

135
00:10:02,310 --> 00:10:04,469
And recursive still works as a name.

136
00:10:04,809 --> 00:10:09,662
Whitespace. A formatter re-indents a YAML
list on one branch, while you rename a

137
00:10:09,702 --> 00:10:10,579
metric on main.

138
00:10:10,919 --> 00:10:14,729
A conflict. Now with -X
ignore-space-change 🟡 CAUTION.

139
00:10:14,769 --> 00:10:19,006
Predict what the merged file
looks like: reformatted or not?

140
00:10:19,046 --> 00:10:20,147
Say it out loud.

141
00:10:24,987 --> 00:10:30,041
The conflict is gone, and so is the
reformatting. The merged file has your two-space

142
00:10:30,081 --> 00:10:35,014
indentation, and the merge printed no
diffstat, because its tree equals your tree.

143
00:10:43,554 --> 00:10:48,885
git merge-file 🟢 SAFE with -p runs the
file-level merge on three ordinary files,

144
00:10:48,925 --> 00:10:52,309
with no repository involved,
and prints the result.

145
00:10:52,349 --> 00:10:56,607
Its exit status is the number of
conflicts. Five pairs of edits.

146
00:10:56,647 --> 00:11:00,971
For each one, say it before the
output: conflict, or no conflict?

147
00:11:01,311 --> 00:11:04,832
One. Ours and theirs change
lines in different regions.

148
00:11:05,172 --> 00:11:07,215
No conflict: both applied.

149
00:11:07,555 --> 00:11:10,221
Two. The same line, different values.

150
00:11:10,561 --> 00:11:11,325
A conflict.

151
00:11:11,665 --> 00:11:14,119
Three. The same line, the same value.

152
00:11:14,459 --> 00:11:17,169
None: applied once, exit status 0.

153
00:11:17,509 --> 00:11:23,573
Four, the one that surprises people. Ours
changes line 2, theirs changes line 3.

154
00:11:23,613 --> 00:11:26,778
Conflict, or no conflict? Say it out loud.

155
00:11:31,618 --> 00:11:35,211
A conflict, although the two
sides changed different lines.

156
00:11:35,251 --> 00:11:37,428
No unchanged line lies between them.

157
00:11:37,768 --> 00:11:41,899
Five. Ours changes line
2, theirs changes line 4.

158
00:11:42,239 --> 00:11:46,851
No conflict. Line 3 is unchanged
on both sides, and that is enough.

159
00:11:56,491 --> 00:11:58,139
Five mistakes to watch for.

160
00:11:58,479 --> 00:12:03,686
Using -s ours where -X ours was meant.
Root cause: one letter; -s ours is a

161
00:12:03,726 --> 00:12:09,563
different strategy that takes none of the
other side's content and still records the

162
00:12:09,603 --> 00:12:09,982
merge.

163
00:12:10,322 --> 00:12:14,152
Using -X ours or -X theirs
to make a red merge green.

164
00:12:14,192 --> 00:12:20,286
Root cause: the option decides every
conflicting hunk silently, including the ones a

165
00:12:20,326 --> 00:12:22,184
human should have decided.

166
00:12:22,524 --> 00:12:26,177
Expecting -X ignore-space-change
to merge a reformatting.

167
00:12:26,217 --> 00:12:31,555
Root cause: where their change is whitespace
only, our version of the line is used,

168
00:12:31,595 --> 00:12:33,693
so the reformatting is discarded.

169
00:12:34,033 --> 00:12:37,276
Being surprised by a conflict
on "different lines".

170
00:12:37,316 --> 00:12:42,234
Root cause: adjacency; two hunks with
no unchanged line between them form one

171
00:12:42,274 --> 00:12:42,684
region.

172
00:12:43,024 --> 00:12:47,773
Assuming a clean merge is a correct merge.
Root cause: Git compares lines of text

173
00:12:47,813 --> 00:12:49,605
and never conflicts on meaning.

174
00:12:51,445 --> 00:12:56,339
Now, out of the lab. Some files conflict
far more often than others, and the

175
00:12:56,379 --> 00:13:00,040
textbook names two kinds that
every AI and ML team knows.

176
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:04,973
A Jupyter notebook stores outputs and
an execution counter next to the code.

177
00:13:05,013 --> 00:13:10,102
So two people who only ran the same
notebook have changed the same lines, and a

178
00:13:10,142 --> 00:13:14,192
conflicted merge can leave a file
that is no longer valid JSON.

179
00:13:14,232 --> 00:13:18,671
And append-only files: changelogs,
migration lists, dependency lists.

180
00:13:18,711 --> 00:13:23,345
Every branch inserts after the same
last line, which is the same region.

181
00:13:23,685 --> 00:13:28,425
For the options, the defensible case: a
regenerated lock file, where one side is

182
00:13:28,465 --> 00:13:33,144
correct by construction. And a reassurance
from the textbook: the audit command

183
00:13:33,184 --> 00:13:38,163
you'll meet in video 37 still shows the
choice, because it repeats the merge without

184
00:13:38,203 --> 00:13:38,939
your options.

185
00:13:40,779 --> 00:13:47,431
Your turn. Do Exercise 6.6, Level 2,
"which of three changes conflicts?", in

186
00:13:47,471 --> 00:13:50,336
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

187
00:13:50,676 --> 00:13:55,821
For each pair of edits, mark the changed
base lines of both sides on paper, as in

188
00:13:55,861 --> 00:14:00,493
the diagram, and decide from overlap
or contact before you run the merge.

189
00:14:00,533 --> 00:14:02,541
Write the prediction down first.

190
00:14:04,381 --> 00:14:09,276
Q127: "Two branches changed different
lines of one file and the merge still

191
00:14:09,316 --> 00:14:13,421
conflicted. Give the exact condition
under which that happens."

192
00:14:15,761 --> 00:14:20,003
Answer out loud. A strong answer states
the condition in terms of hunks and

193
00:14:20,043 --> 00:14:24,398
unchanged lines, gives the smallest
example of each side of the boundary, and

194
00:14:24,438 --> 00:14:28,794
explains why Git draws the boundary
there. Say what you would do about such a

195
00:14:28,834 --> 00:14:30,849
conflict, and what you would not do.

196
00:14:32,689 --> 00:14:38,476
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say: a strategy is the merge algorithm,

197
00:14:38,516 --> 00:14:42,811
chosen with -s. A strategy option,
given with -X, adjusts it.

198
00:14:42,851 --> 00:14:46,434
ort is the default, and
recursive is now a synonym.

199
00:14:46,474 --> 00:14:51,835
-X ours and -X theirs do a real merge
and decide only the conflicting hunks.

200
00:14:52,175 --> 00:14:57,228
-s ours discards the other side's content
entirely and still records the merge.

201
00:14:57,268 --> 00:15:02,515
There is no -s theirs. A whitespace option
uses our version of a line where theirs

202
00:15:02,555 --> 00:15:07,544
changed only whitespace. And two edits
conflict when they change the same base

203
00:15:07,584 --> 00:15:12,380
lines, or lines with no unchanged line
between them, unless the changes are

204
00:15:12,420 --> 00:15:13,024
identical.

205
00:15:14,864 --> 00:15:18,624
Read sections 8.6 and 8.7 of Chapter 8.

206
00:15:18,964 --> 00:15:26,209
Challenge: Exercise 6.10, Level 5, "the
weekly sync that conflicts more every week",

207
00:15:26,249 --> 00:15:29,331
in exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

208
00:15:29,671 --> 00:15:34,169
You can now tell apart two commands that
differ by one letter, and predict a

209
00:15:34,209 --> 00:15:38,348
conflict from two edits on paper. Do
the exercise with a pencil first.

210
00:15:38,388 --> 00:15:43,363
Next time: the anatomy of a conflict, in
the working tree, the index and the dot git

211
00:15:43,403 --> 00:15:47,961
folder, and the conflict styles. Until
then, look at the state first and type

212
00:15:48,001 --> 00:15:49,871
second. See you in the next one.
